WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:01.589 align:start position:0%
 
now  dop leader sir jeffrey john donaldson  has urged boris johnson to prioritize  the political crisis in northern ireland  over controversies in downing street  earlier this week northern ireland's  first minister resigned over the  government's failure to address unionist  concerns about the brexit border in the  irish sea  city donaldson said the fallout from  lockdown parties number 10 was making it  difficult for the prime minister to  focus  he's been speaking to our senior ireland  correspondent david blevins  boris johnson promised your party there  would never be a border in the irish sea  then he promised to remove that border  why do you still trust him at all  well the prime minister did led us down  and led us down badly and at the  conservative party conference this year  i met the prime minister he apologized  to me the problem david is that we've  had a lot of words about triggering  article 16 which the protocol  allows the government to do in  circumstances whether it's economic or  societal or political harm caused by the  protocol and yet we haven't had any  action i've asked for a commitment that  if we don't get agreement article 16  will be triggered and the uk government  will take unilateral action to  honor the commitment the prime minister  gave to honor the commitment the  government gave at the restoration of  our devolved institutions that they  would take steps to introduce  legislation to protect northern  ireland's police within the uk and they  have failed to do that that is why i  have acted now  so you've acted the agriculture minister  from your party halted checks we know  that's not subject to judicial review  but if a minister in the devolved  government had the power to halt brexit  checks  how did westminster have the power to  remove northern ireland from the  european union in the first place well  because the people of the united kingdom  voted for all of the united kingdom to  leave the european union but doesn't  that mean the government of the united  kingdom alone has the power to either  halt or continue brexit checks i believe  that the government of the united  kingdom does have the power under  article 16 of the protocol to take  unilateral action to protect northern  ireland's place within the uk and to  protect the integrity of the uk internal  market and i'm saying to them very  clearly that if and i want to see  political stability in northern ireland  david i've worked for decades for  political stability that is my objective  but we can't have political stability  when the concerns of unionists are being  completely ignored when this protocol is  harming our relationship with the rest  of the uk and the government is failing  to act  when do you want to see the stormont  election and if the dup  doesn't win that election are you  prepared to nominate a deputy first  minister and cia shin fan first minister  well it is a matter for the secretary of  state for northern ireland to decide the  timing of the election  we will be ready to take our case to the  people as soon as that election is  called in terms of the outcome of the  election david i want unionism to win i  am not contemplating uh defeat uh at the  hands of sinn fein my objective is to  win the support of people in northern  ireland to maintain um the dup and  unionism as the largest block in the  northern ireland assembly that's what i  want to achieve and i think in those  circumstances we will have a unionist  first minister so what do you say to  people who would fear that the dup will  just use the protocol right to prevent  sharing power with sinn fein  i'm committed to devolution i am  committed to sharing power with other  parties in northern ireland  but we cannot go on with a situation  where the views and concerns of unionism  and unionist parties in northern are  being ignored i want to restore fairness  i want to restore respect i want to  restore political stability in northern  ireland but that means the protocol has  to be dealt with solutions need to be  found and the sooner that happens the  better and i hope that before the  assembly election david we will get an  agreement uh on uh dealing with the  problems and resolving  the difficulties created by this rec  border the ousting of arling foster  revealed division in the dup the parties  had three leaders unlike two first  ministers in the space of ten months is  the party in meltdown i don't think that  it is david we're um preparing for the  election i've worked hard since i became  leader of the dup and to heal the wounds  um like every political party from time  to time um the dup has its differences  sadly last year those differences became  a very public affair  and i deeply regret that that happened  and i am working  to pull the party together and get the  party into shape to fight the election  and win the election for unionism so  geoffrey the founder of this party the  reverend ian paisley  would not have been impressed by boozy  parties in downing street  why is the dup the only opposition party  not calling for the prime minister's  resignation  well to be clear we have spoken out very  strongly  and i think that one of the most  passionate speeches that we've seen  in the house of commons was that of my  colleague jim shannon the member of  parliament for strangford who spoke  powerfully about the experience of his  family losing his mother-in-law  during the covent pandemic  but i would say this to the prime  minister  northern ireland is an important part of  the united kingdom  you're our prime minister and if you  want to  build confidence  then  do what other prime ministers did in the  past recognize that we have a serious  problem here and instead of being  focused on  what's going on in downing street be the  prime minister the people needs reach  out to northern ireland help us to  resolve these issues make this a  priority let's get  our political institutions restored on  the basis  of sound foundations that means dealing  with the protocol making these issues a  priority so that the people of northern  ireland  can enjoy strong stable government  that's what we expect of a prime  minister we can't go on like this david  we can't go on with the situation where  serious problems like we have here in  northern are not getting the attention  they deserve those are strong words from  a unionist party leader are you  suggesting that boris johnson  has made northern ireland such  a low priority that the peace process is  effectively being damaged i'm saying  that all of the controversy surrounding  um  downing street is making it difficult  for the prime minister to focus on what  needs to get priority i recognize the  situation for example in ukraine is  critical i recognize that getting the uk  out of uh covert and and into a more  normal situation and the recovery of our  economy and are supporting our health  service are important but so is northern  ireland and whilst i welcome very much  the commitment of liz trust  to drive forward the negotiations it  disappoints me that our prime minister  cannot give a firm commitment  today  that if  there is not agreement reached uh with  the european union he will take the  steps that are necessary to restore  northern ireland's place within the uk  internal market that means triggering  article or 16. that's the commitment i  need to get from this prime minister and  if he doesn't give it to you there's not  going to be a power sharing government  regardless of what happens during the  election well if the prime minister  as i do cherishes the union  and i believe that the prime minister is  a unionist  then he needs to recognize that right  now northern ireland um  there are  serious problems here that need to be  addressed i want the prime minister to  give that priority i want the prime  minister to give a firm commitment no  more words we need action here in  northern ireland sir jeffrey donaldson  thank you very much

